{0 Rocq Alignment Boundary}

This page records how Kairos should stay alignable with the existing Rocq
formalization and with the mathematical formalization intended for the paper.
The direction is fixed:

{[
Rocq formalization
  -> paper mathematical presentation
  -> Kairos implementation alignment boundary
]}

The OCaml implementation does not invent the proof architecture. It must
expose the semantic objects that correspond to the Rocq proof architecture,
and it must mark everything else as derived view, backend, diagnostic, or
external tooling.
Syntactic sugar is also outside this Rocq core: observers, history
definitions, concise assignments, and other surface conveniences must be
elaborated before the Rocq-facing boundary.

The machine-readable source of truth is
[docs/rocq_alignment_manifest.json]. It records:

- the Rocq repository, paper branch, and immutable commit used for alignment;
- the public theorem entry points;
- the Rocq concepts that the paper should present;
- the Kairos modules that must expose matching artifacts;
- the implementation details that are outside the Rocq core.

The field-by-field audit is [docs/rocq_projection_audit.json]. Its conclusion
is architectural: Kairos needs explicit product-summary and step-contract
views so that the proof-relevant objects can be inspected before backend
encoding. These views are derived from the semantic boundary; they are not the
formal source of the Rocq or paper theorem. The decision is recorded in
[docs/architecture/decisions/ADR-0010-explicit-rocq-alignment-projections.md].

The current principle-level adequacy audit is
{!page-rocq_ocaml_adequacy}. It records which Rocq objects already match the
OCaml implementation, which correspondences need a preservation lemma, and
which proof-relevant gaps must be resolved before claiming full adequacy.

{1 Rocq Source}

The current Rocq source recorded by the manifest is:

{[
/Users/fdabrowski/Repos/kairos/kairos-spec/kairos-rocq
branch lab/rocq-paper-core
commit f3facc051901245e33a4d79676fcff6fcd464087
]}

The paper branch is treated as a frozen source reference.  The Kairos checks do
not require the local Rocq checkout to be on that branch.  They verify instead
that the recorded branch still points to the recorded commit and that the
recorded theorem files and names exist at that commit.  This keeps ordinary
Kairos development independent from Rocq work branches while preventing silent
drift of the paper reference.

The public theorem cut used by the paper is centered on:

- [local_obligations_sound] in [theories/PaperCore/Soundness.v];
- [generated_obligations_sound] in
  [theories/PaperCore/GeneratedObligations.v];
- [concrete_environment_generated_obligations_sound] in
  [theories/PaperCore/ConcreteAdequacy.v];
- [true_annotations_have_semantic_witness] in
  [theories/PaperCore/RelativeCompleteness.v].

The core theorem shape is:

{[
GeneratedObligationsValid
  -> contract_valid
]}

The paper formalization should present this structure mathematically, with
notation adapted for readability but with the same proof cuts.

The internal Rocq module organization should preserve this cut.  In particular,
definition modules may introduce the mathematical predicates used by the paper,
while construction-correctness modules may contain the local singleton, fold, or
lookup facts needed to prove those predicates for the generated objects.  Those
local facts are not additional paper principles and should not force new final
theorems.

{1 What Kairos Must Expose}

Kairos must make the following proof-relevant units identifiable:

- normalized source program and well-formedness assumptions;
- the boundary between surface elaboration checks and the normalized
  Rocq-facing core program;
- instrumented runs, observations, and temporal slots;
- supplied assumption and guarantee automata, plus the implementation point
  where their product-level normal form is validated;
- product-state and product-step anchors;
- step classification into safe, bad-assumption, and bad-guarantee cases;
- product-summary proof-step summaries;
- product-summary clause families and their coverage;
- step-contract extraction and lowering;
- generated safe, unsafe, and initialization obligations;
- the canonical obligation set before Why3 grouping, scheduling, or solver
  execution.

These are not optional diagnostics. They are the implementation-side handles
needed to state that Kairos matches the Rocq theorem and the paper
formalization.

{1 Proof-Principle Cut}

The current Rocq proof has a useful pipeline cut:

{[
source program + supplied automata
  -> automata normal-form validation at the product boundary
  -> product summaries and clause families
       (ProofStepSummary + SummaryClauseFamilies)
  -> step contracts
       (StepContract)
  -> generated obligation validity
  -> global automata validity
]}

In Kairos, this means:

- [Product_build], [From_model], [Pre], [Post], and [Temporal_lower] are
  proof-relevant reference stages;
- [Canonical_obligations] is the reference boundary that names the canonical
  Stage 1 clauses and Stage 2 contracts before export, backend grouping, or
  Why3 lowering;
- [Product_reachability] is a proof-relevant extension only if it is justified
  as a general invariant principle, not as pruning;
- [Proof_kernel_types] and related [proof_export] modules are projection
  candidates that may witness product-summary objects, but they are not
  automatically the theorem source;
- Why3 modules are backend realization territory: the step-contract extraction
  and lowering relation is proof-relevant, while helper names, grouping,
  worker counts, dumps, and solver statuses are not.

The derived OCaml views are now split into:

- [Product_summary_projection], which exposes product-summary identity,
  requirements, runtime requirements, ensures, and safe/unsafe cases;
- [Kernel_clause_projection], which exposes [KernelClause], [TimedFact],
  product anchors and classified clause families before proof-export lowering;
- [Obligation_family_projection], which classifies generated clauses by proof
  schema and exposes the Rocq-facing [SummaryClauseFamilies] predicates;
- [Step_contract_projection], which exposes pre-Why3 step contracts with
  summary identity and covered cases as a grouped view of
  [Canonical_obligations.stage2].

The `proof_export` data model is still not enough by itself:

- [Ir.product_step_summary] has most summary logic but lacks explicit steps
  and clause families;
- [Proof_kernel_types.proof_step_summary_ir] has steps and clauses but lacks
  first-class summary identity, requires, ensures, safe cases, and unsafe
  cases;
- [Proof_kernel_types.generated_clause_ir] is now only a typed alias to
  [Kernel_clause_projection.classified_clause] with a proof-export codec; it is
  not a second generated-clause data model;
- the Why3 compiler consumes [Step_contract_projection] directly rather than
  defining a second backend-shaped [StepContract] projection.

Therefore the implementation-side Rocq/paper correspondence should go through
[Canonical_obligations] for the proof principle, then explain which derived
views expose or group those objects before diagnostics and Why3 emission.

{1 Paper Consequence}

The POPL paper should not present an OCaml-centric pipeline first and then try
to retrofit Rocq. It should present the Rocq-backed mathematical objects:

- program semantics and observation traces;
- contract automata and assume-guarantee validity;
- product anchors and summaries;
- local obligations and step contracts;
- soundness from generated obligations.

The implementation section can then explain how Kairos exposes and realizes
these objects, and which parts are deliberately outside the theorem.

{1 Enforcement}

The alignment is checked by:

- [scripts/check_rocq_alignment_manifest.py], which verifies the recorded
  Rocq paper branch/commit, theorem files at that commit, theorem names, and
  Kairos paths;
- [scripts/check_reference_pipeline_boundaries.py], which verifies the
  implementation-side reference/projection/backend classification;
- [tests/check_reference_stability.sh], which checks that backend-only options
  do not change canonical reference views.

These checks are not proofs. They keep the implementation architecture clean
enough that the adequacy proof obligation remains visible instead of being
buried in Why3, dumps, or diagnostics.
